**Claim (Original L/C uncertainty in angular frequency)** · `claim:nrz:lc-closing-angular-uncertainty`

LC closing requires the original L/C deterministic enclosures and complete declared joint model to reach the published angular output under (`rule:nrz:lc-angular-frequency-publication`). The native original-center tests and common/fixed-source twins cover this obligation with inherited `LinearizedMoments`; statistical covariance is neither a physical support interval nor an exact nonlinear moment. Original-source composition keeps its separate admitted boundary (`rule:nrz:matrix-core-lc-source-composition`).

**Claim (Shared voltage covariance inside the driven plan)** · `claim:nrz:lc-closing-driven-voltage-covariance`

LC closing requires the exact common-voltage cross block inside the same driven plan under (`rule:nrz:lc-resonance-plan-strata`), (`req:nrz:lc-plan-completion-evidence`). The independent gains and negative cross covariance of (`rule:nrz:lc-resonance-phasor-domains`) already have both-format component tests; assembling them with stable driven rows and a missing resonant-current block remains plan work.

**Claim (Driven uncertain L/C follows closing)** · `claim:nrz:lc-closing-driven-lineage-boundary`

Original uncertain L/C-to-reactance lineage follows LC closing under (`rule:nrz:matrix-core-lc-driven-lineage`). Closing uses the explicit fixed-L/C shared-voltage experiment and the separate uncertain autonomous experiment, because their independently checked assurance does not supply the nonlinear cross-frequency source composition. A later adapter must meet the existing rule before broadening that driven capability; neither missing correlation nor a fixed-direction omission is silently zero.

**Claim (Generic root moments follow closing)** · `claim:nrz:lc-closing-root-companion-boundary`

Generic heap root moment companions follow LC closing under (`rule:nrz:matrix-core-lc-root-companions`). The typed operator/root and native frequency publications already carry the statistics needed by this target; arbitrary `MomentRef` composition needs its own atomic owner and both-target evidence, so its absence does not block the closed consumers or imply generic support.

