## One LC relation in every direction · `sec:nrz:lc-relation`

**Definition (Original autonomous LC relation)** · `def:nrz:lc-relation`

The positive-amplitude branch of the source-free model (`def:nrz:lc-resonance-model`) relates `L>0`, `C>0`, a starting phase `theta0`, any declared phase-time pairs `(t_k,theta_k)`, positive voltage/current amplitudes `Vhat,Ihat` and energy `E>0` by `omega0=(1 rad)/sqrt(L*C)`, `theta_k = theta0 + omega0*t_k mod (2*pi rad)`, `E=C*Vhat^2/2=L*Ihat^2/2`, and `Vhat/Ihat=sqrt(L/C)`. These are one simultaneous relation; querying another quantity changes the projection of its feasible set, not the original equations or their authority. The full-cycle angle and the positive roots retain their exact definitions under (`rule:nrz:lc-resonance-kinds`).

**Rule (Relation quantity meanings and the zero-energy branch)** · `rule:nrz:lc-relation-kinds`

L carries henries and inductance kind; C farads and capacitance kind; each t seconds and duration kind relative to an explicitly declared common epoch; theta0 and theta_k radians and angle-difference kind; Vhat volts and voltage kind; Ihat amperes and current kind; E joules and energy kind. Amplitudes are nonnegative magnitudes, not signed instantaneous values or the driven source amplitude. The derived product L*C has seconds squared and duration_squared kind; its positive square root has seconds and duration kind. The amplitude ratio has ohms and impedance kind, omega0 has rad/s and angular-frequency kind, and a=omega0/(1 rad) has inverse seconds and rate kind. Oscillation phase is distinct from the impedance argument in (`rule:nrz:lc-resonance-kinds`), though both are angles modulo a full turn. At E=0 the separate quiescent branch has Vhat=Ihat=0; the ratio and physical oscillation phase are undefined. It returns that domain outcome, never evaluates 0/0 and never presents an arbitrary phase clock as a measured oscillation. The positive L/C law still determines a natural frequency; a numeric phase observation requires the positive-amplitude branch.

**Rule (Supply and query arbitrary quantity combinations)** · `rule:nrz:lc-relation-any-direction`

Every relation quantity may be supplied exactly, as an interval, or with declared uncertainty, and any subset may be queried, including a supplied quantity for consistency. The same checked relation must support L/C-to-phase queries, pairs-to-LC projection, pairs plus one element-to-the-other-element and future-phase queries, pairs plus amplitude ratio-to-both-elements queries, and a full-input consistency check. Pairs plus energy must also be accepted, with the free scaling direction honestly retained unless an amplitude or another scale constraint removes it (`arg:nrz:lc-relation-energy-identifiability`). No direction-specific solver may replace the original relation, omit an input constraint, or embed the reference answers. Checked algebraic eliminations and root/branch projections retain all original positivity and definedness premises.

**Rule (Certified interval answers and uncertainty admission)** · `rule:nrz:lc-relation-certified-sets`

For the original admitted constraints let S be the complete feasible relation set. An answer to quantity tuple Q is a certified outer set O, expressed as a union of boxes with `projection_Q(S) subset O`, retaining the original constraints and branch associations so box products do not invent independence. An exact answer is a singleton or an enclosure of an exact irrational with its derivation, not an uncertified point estimate. Statistical likelihoods and confidence regions are outside this interface. A value with a deterministic uncertainty bound supplies an interval; standard uncertainty or covariance alone supplies no finite support, and must retain an explicit unbounded physical domain or a separately declared deterministic support assumption. No default confidence multiplier converts moments into bounds (`def:nrz:decision-node`). Timing uncertainty belongs to the input boxes and remains coupled to the same phase/frequency relation; shared clock errors retain their original joint constraints.

**Rule (Independent and shared measurement uncertainty)** · `rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`

Every measured time, phase, amplitude, energy or element value may carry two separately declared deterministic layers: its own per-sample interval, and one bounded systematic error shared by all samples of that quantity. For an additive error the physical value is `q_i=x_i+s_q`, with each `x_i` in its own interval and a single `s_q` in its declared interval; for a multiplicative calibration such as amplitude gain it is `q_i=g_q*x_i`, with the gain domain and physical positivity checked explicitly. Either layer may be absent (zero offset or unit gain), or both may be present. These are independent uncertainty layers, not statistical independence assumptions. Retain the joint domain, original equations and each sample association: a shared error is never replaced by independent sample boxes, and identical per-sample boxes do not assert a shared error. Any marginal relaxation is labelled outer coverage only; a relaxed point establishes feasibility only after checking every original equation and both uncertainty layers (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`). Clock rate drift remains the separate boundary (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-excludes-clock-drift`).

**Observation (Measured uncertainty attachment boundary)** · `obs:nrz:lc-relation-measurement-attachments`

At source baseline `e861450`, `LcInput` and `LcInterval` in `crates/bindings/src/lc_relation/` retain each original deterministic interval, including repeated declarations, and `witness/check.rs` checks exact tuples against them. `pairs/admit.rs` intersects per-sample time and lifted phase declarations but refuses a timestamp that remains nonsingleton; lifted phase intervals already participate jointly in rate/initial-phase elimination. `clock.rs` and `clock/{admit,project,witness}.rs` retain one bounded shared offset through `beta=a*offset`, currently with exact zero physical initial phase and timestamp supports containing the full nominal-plus-offset images. The shared-clock independent-box control is (`[MATRIX-test:integration:lc-relation-shared-clock-independent-box-control]`). `pairs/scale.rs` admits only exact positive amplitude, energy and element values; `capacitance.rs` separately retains one positive capacitor interval jointly with rate. General per-sample timing recovery, shared phase offsets, amplitude intervals/shared gain, energy intervals and shared element errors are not supplied by those adapters. These measured attachment points delimit implementation of (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`); declaration and point membership alone do not establish an interval projection.

**Rule (Conservative per-sample timestamp coverage)** · `rule:nrz:lc-relation-timing-outer-implementation`

The independent timing layer of (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`) has a conservative adapter (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:lc-relation-timing-outer]`). For retained positive rational rate support A and each error box D_i, exact corner extrema give `H_i=hull{a*delta:a in A,delta in D_i}`; `alpha+a*nominal_i` then belongs to the original lifted phase interval minus H_i. Original feasibility implies these relaxed nominal-time constraints at the same rate and cycle, so the nominal checker's complete cycle proof supplies outer coverage. The reverse implication does not hold because the independent hull discarded rate/error correlation. Only a full tuple checked against every original equation and time box is a feasibility witness. Unequal errors and distinct shared-offset semantics have executable controls (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-outer-corners]`), (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-outer-shared-control]`); a relaxed boundary rate that fails original containment is (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-outer-witness-boundary]`). Exact timing elimination follows (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-timing-exact-implementation`); composition of both time layers follows (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-timing-both-implementation`).

**Rule (Exact per-sample timestamp domain and restoration)** · `rule:nrz:lc-relation-timing-exact-implementation`

For each proposed integer lift, retain the original rational lifted phase box `[p_min,p_max]`, timestamp box `[t_min,t_max]` and positive rate support. Existence of an original time is equivalent to `p_min-a*t_max<=alpha<=p_max-a*t_min`. The exact adapter (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:lc-relation-timing-exact]`) keeps this joint `(alpha,a)` domain and eliminates it with the existing correlated strip checker. At `t_max` add the lower face p_min and redundant upper face `p_max+a_max*(t_max-t_min)`; at `t_min` add the upper face p_max and redundant lower face `p_min-a_max*(t_max-t_min)`. Redundancy follows from `0<a<=a_max`. Lift and intersect the original phase endpoints once, retain their prepared full-turn shifts, and construct rational auxiliary faces. For a base proposal b, every original cycle n maps to the duplicated auxiliary difference `n-b`; checking the zero auxiliary proposal therefore proves complete coverage of the original proposal. This coordinate translation avoids rebuilding irrational phases on redundant faces; the initial reconstruction also reuses the already checked auxiliary witness. An unknown initial phase retains every common integer gauge, not a selected representative only.

For each retained rate and local initial phase, reconstruct every timestamp in `[t_min,t_max] intersect [(p_min-alpha)/a,(p_max-alpha)/a]`; positive division and the two faces prove that intersection nonempty. Restore original phase representatives, scale constraints and cycles, then check every original equation and input (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-exact-original-inputs]`). Independent endpoint witnesses and a crossed-marginal refusal establish the retained correlation (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-exact-recovery]`), (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-exact-joint-phase]`). Because each exact tuple also maps to the corner relaxation, its projected domain is contained in the conservative cover (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-timing-outer-implementation`); generated rational controls and a strict-inclusion fixture check this (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-exact-tightens-outer]`). Nonzero original lifts, overlapping zero-crossing time supports and unresolved-cycle/resource refusals remain explicit (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-exact-lifted-cycles]`), (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-exact-zero-crossing]`), (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-exact-refusals-work]`). Joint shared-plus-independent timing follows (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-timing-both-implementation`); timing-aware horizons and refinement remain separate.

**Rule (Both timestamp uncertainty layers retained jointly)** · `rule:nrz:lc-relation-timing-both-implementation`

`LcSharedTiming` retains the full model `t_i=nominal_i+offset+delta_i`, with one offset domain and separate per-sample error intervals (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:lc-relation-timing-both]`). The effective coordinate `beta=alpha+a*offset` satisfies the optional physical-phase/shared-offset faces while independent sample times stay in `nominal_i+D_i`; exact per-sample elimination retains all these constraints in one joint domain (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-both-timing-initial-phase-implementation`). Original total time boxes intersect the declared images while retaining both time-error layers (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-narrower-both-timing-implementation`). Restoring the same offset into every effective timestamp and restoring physical initial phase `beta-a*offset` gives the original equations, which are then checked together with every `delta_i` box. No marginal hull replaces either uncertainty layer.

With nominal measured pairs `(t,theta)=(1,2)` and `(2,4)`, rate support `[1,3]`, per-sample errors `[-1/100,1/100]` and `[-1/50,1/50]`, and common offset `[-1/10,1/10]`, the combined rate domain is `[200/103,200/97]`. The independent layer alone gives `[200/101,200/99]` and the shared layer alone gives the singleton 2. Independent total timestamp boxes instead give `[100/53,100/47]` and admit a rate-21/10 physical tuple excluded by the joint model (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-both-layers]`), (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-both-marginal-control]`). The original bounds, offset support and caller Work remain checked (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-timing-both-refusals-work]`). Unknown physical initial phase and its actual-time horizon now follow (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-both-timing-initial-phase-implementation`); narrower original total times follow (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-narrower-both-timing-implementation`); reported-clock horizons follow (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-reported-clock-horizons-implementation`); single-clock and both-time-layer refinement follow (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-clock-refinement-implementation`); drift stays excluded (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-excludes-clock-drift`).

**Rule (Both phase uncertainty layers retained jointly)** · `rule:nrz:lc-relation-phase-both-implementation`

`LcSharedPhase` implements actual phase `theta_i=x_i+s`, with each measured representative in its own finite interval and a single finite rational additive offset s (`[BINDINGS-rule:bindings:lc-relation-phase-both]`). The exact coordinate change `beta=alpha-s` permits the independent timing solver to retain the complete rate/beta domain. A supplied physical initial-phase interval A imposes `beta in A-S`; every retained beta still has the nonempty fibre `S intersect (A-beta)`. Without supplied A, the common integer gauge remains unrestricted. At a retained rate, physical initial phase has fibre `(B+S) intersect A`, with A omitted when unknown; for selected physical alpha the offset fibre is `S intersect (alpha-B)`. These fibres retain the correlation that marginal phase boxes would discard. Original phase constraints must contain each complete sample-plus-offset image; narrower restrictions refuse. Restored witnesses add the same offset to actual initial phase and every physical observation phase, then check all original equations and each measured interval.

For nominal measured pairs `(t,x)=(1,2)` and `(2,4)`, independent phase errors `[-1/100,1/100]` and `[-1/50,1/50]`, shared offset `[-1/10,1/10]` and physical initial phase zero, the combined rate domain is `[197/100,203/100]`. Independent errors alone give `[199/100,201/100]`, the shared offset alone gives the singleton 2, and independent total phase boxes give `[97/50,103/50]` (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-phase-both-layers]`), (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-phase-marginal-control]`). Unknown or bounded physical initial phase keeps its offset fibre (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-phase-initial-offset-fibre]`); independent timestamp uncertainty also composes exactly (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-phase-uncertain-times]`). Admission and Work refusal controls remain explicit (`[BINDINGS-test:integration:lc-relation-phase-refusals-work]`). This increment retains independent time uncertainty; simultaneous shared clock and shared phase offsets remain separate.

